Month = {JUL 4},
Abstract = {Tensions and unrest fuelled by ecological crises hint at a permeable
   relationship between environmental conditions and violence. However, the
   internal violent conflict literature, focused on determining causal
   relations, often omits the presence of social and symbolic processes in
   the interactions between humans and nature. Non-violent environmental
   conflict and environmental politics studies acknowledge such a complex
   and politically contingent relation and the various ecological
   interpretations that societies generate. This paper bridges those
   approaches and offers a framework to extend Foucault-inspired
   environmental discourse analysis to cases of internal violence. It
   recommends moving away from essentialist examinations of the
   environment-violence nexus and from causally focused research to examine
   the mechanisms of meaning, political contingency and construction of
   eco-social issues. The guerra del agua (December 1999-April 2000) in
   Cochabamba (Bolivia) illustrates how discursive mechanisms are
   entrenched in a conflict over natural resource governance. My analysis
   shows how politicising an ecological issue through social justice and
   rights discourse can affect a conflict's power balance. I study how
   competing knowledge and meaning systems regarding environmental issues
   permeate the power structures of violent political contention. Through
   environmental constructions, actors develop discourses of legitimacy,
   community and internal mobilisation, and external support to alter the
   conflict's power dynamics.},
